    39	---
    40	
    41	## Definition
    42	
    43	A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is **locally
    44	compact** when
    45	
    46	> every point of $X$ has a compact neighbourhood:
    47	
    48	that is, for every $x \in X$ there is a neighbourhood $N$ of $x$
    49	([[def-neighbourhood-top]]) that is a compact subset of $X$
    50	([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    51	
    52	**A neighbourhood need not be open here**, and that is what makes the condition
    53	the weak one it is meant to be: $N$ is required only to contain some open set
    54	containing $x$. Writing "compact **open** neighbourhood" instead would define a
    55	strictly stronger property, satisfied by no space in which a point has no compact
    56	open neighbourhood, $\mathbb{R}$ among them; and requiring the compact set merely
    57	to *contain* $x$ would define a property so weak that every space with a
    58	singleton has it, singletons being compact.
    59	
    60	**Every compact space is locally compact**, since $X$ itself is a neighbourhood
    61	of each of its points and is a compact subset of itself. The converse fails, and
    62	$\mathbb{R}^n$ is the standard witness.
    63	
    64	**What the condition says in a metric space.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space
    65	carrying its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metrizable-space]]),
    66	with balls as in [[def-metric-ball]], and let $x \in X$. Then
    67	
    68	> $x$ has a compact neighbourhood **if and only if** there are a real $r > 0$ and
    69	> a compact $K \subseteq X$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq K$.
    70	
    71	*Both directions are immediate and are discharged here.* If $N$ is a compact
    72	neighbourhood of $x$, fix an open $U$ with $x \in U \subseteq N$; by
    73	[[def-metric-topology]] there is $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U \subseteq N$, so
    74	$K := N$ serves. Conversely, if $B(x,r) \subseteq K$ with $K$ compact, then $K$
    75	contains the open set $B(x,r)$, which contains $x$, so $K$ is a neighbourhood of
    76	$x$ and is compact. Compactness of a subset of $(X,d)$ means the same thing read
    77	metrically and read topologically
    78	([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]), so the criterion may be
    79	applied with either development's theorems.
    80	
    81	**$\mathbb{R}^n$ is locally compact for every $n \ge 1$.** Give $\mathbb{R}^n$
    82	the product topology, which is the metric topology of $d_\infty$
    83	([[lem-metrics-on-rn]], [[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]). For
    84	$p \in \mathbb{R}^n$ the set
    85	
    86	$$Q_p \;:=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^n : d_\infty(x,p) \le 1 \,\}$$
    87	
    88	is closed, being the complement of the union of the open balls $B(y, d_\infty(y,p) - 1)$
    89	over the points $y$ with $d_\infty(y,p) > 1$, and it is bounded
    90	([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]), lying inside $B(p, 2)$; so $Q_p$ is compact by
    91	[[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]. It contains the open ball $B(p,1)$,
    92	which contains $p$, so it is a compact neighbourhood of $p$. The space
    93	$\mathbb{R}^n$ is not compact, so local compactness is strictly weaker than
    94	compactness.
    95	
    96	## Remarks
    97	
    98	**Local compactness is a local condition and compactness is not.** The definition
    99	quantifies over points and asks for something in a neighbourhood of each; nothing
   100	is asserted about covers of the whole space. That is why a locally compact space
   101	may be as large as one likes, and why the two properties separate.
   102	
   103	**Where the extra strength is needed.** For an arbitrary space, "every point has
   104	a compact neighbourhood" does not by itself give a *base* of compact
   105	neighbourhoods at each point, nor an open set with compact closure around each
   106	compact set. Both of those do follow once the space is also Hausdorff, and that
   107	is [[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]]; several authors build the stronger
   108	condition into the definition and then note the agreement in the Hausdorff case.
   109	This library takes the weak definition and proves the strengthening under the
   110	hypothesis that licenses it.
   111	
   112	**Local compactness is not hereditary**, unlike metrizability. A subspace of a
   113	locally compact space need not be locally compact, and
   114	[[fs-local-compactness-is-hereditary]] records the failure with a witness; what
   115	does survive is heredity along open and along closed subspaces of a locally
   116	compact **Hausdorff** space ([[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]]).
     1	---
