    27	
    28	## Definition
    29	
    30	A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is a **Baire
    31	space** when
    32	
    33	> for every sequence $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of subsets of $X$ that are open
    34	> and dense in $X$ ([[def-dense-top]], [[def-sequence-convergence-top]],
    35	> [[def-natural-numbers]]), the intersection $\bigcap_{n \in \mathbb{N}} U_n$ is
    36	> dense in $X$.
    37	
    38	As everywhere in this library $\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$, so the sequence starts
    39	at $U_0$ and an index range copied from a text that starts at $1$ must be shifted
    40	before it is used here.
    41	
    42	**The condition unwound.** Denseness of $A \subseteq X$ says $\overline{A} = X$
    43	([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]), equivalently that $A$ meets every
    44	nonempty open subset of $X$ ([[def-dense-top]]). So the Baire condition reads:
    45	
    46	> for every sequence $(U_n)$ of dense open sets and every nonempty open
    47	> $W \subseteq X$ there is a point of $W$ lying in every $U_n$.
    48	
    49	That is the form every proof below uses, and it is the form in which the
    50	condition is checked: one produces a single point, in a given nonempty open set,
    51	belonging to all of the $U_n$ at once.
    52	
    53	**The intersection itself need not be open.** Nothing in the definition asserts
    54	that $\bigcap_n U_n$ is open, and in general it is not; what is asserted is only
    55	that it is dense. A finite intersection of dense open sets is dense and open, and
    56	that much holds in every space; the content of the definition is entirely in the
    57	passage to a countable family.
    58	
    59	**Why the meager formulation is not given here.** The usual equivalent phrasing
    60	says that a countable union of nowhere dense sets has empty interior, or that a
    61	nonempty open set is not meager. This page does not state it, because the
    62	notions of nowhere dense and meager available to it are
    63	[[def-nowhere-dense-meager]], which is stated for subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ and not
    64	for subsets of an arbitrary topological space; restating them here in general
    65	would create a second notion under the same name. The definition above is stated
    66	in terms of denseness alone, which [[def-dense-top]] does define for an arbitrary
    67	space, and it is equivalent to the meager formulation wherever both are
    68	available.
    69	
    70	## Remarks
    71	
    72	**What the definition is for.** It isolates exactly the hypothesis under which a
    73	countable family of "large" open sets still has a large intersection, and that is
    74	the hypothesis a great many existence arguments run on: to produce an object with
    75	countably many properties, one shows that each property is enjoyed by a dense open
    76	set and then takes a point of the intersection. Nothing about the ambient space is
    77	assumed here beyond the definition, so the notion applies to spaces with no metric
    78	and no countability property.
    79	
    80	**Which spaces satisfy it, and at what cost.** Every locally compact Hausdorff
    81	space is a Baire space, assuming dependent choice
    82	([[thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff]]); the corresponding statement
    83	for complete metric spaces, and the exact choice principles the several versions
    84	of the Baire category theorem are equivalent to over ZF, are recorded in
    85	[[rem-baire-category-choice-strength]], which this library states and does not
    86	prove.

