   105	
   106	- **The subspace topology is what makes a subset a space.** Before it, a statement
   107	  such as "the restriction of $f$ to $C$ is continuous" has no meaning, because
   108	  $C$ carries no topology. Every restriction below is taken with respect to the
   109	  subspace topology and with no other convention available.
   110	
   111	- **Openness and closedness are not absolute.** $[0,1)$ is open in $[0,2)$ and is
   112	  neither open nor closed in $\mathbb{R}$; the interval $(0,1)$ is closed in
   113	  itself. A sentence of the form "$A$ is open" is incomplete unless the space is
   114	  named, and this library names it whenever more than one is in play.
   115	
   116	- **Transitivity.** If $S \subseteq T \subseteq X$ then the subspace topology $S$
   117	  inherits from $(T, \mathcal{T}_T)$ is the subspace topology it inherits from
   118	  $X$, since $(U \cap T) \cap S = U \cap S$ for $U \in \mathcal{T}$. So no
   119	  ambiguity arises from the route by which a subset is reached.

